The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says the reports merged into #4471 are one bug with one fix.
They are not. Read report by report, the ticket holds three different problems. Each one has its
own symptom, its own cause and its own fix, and the parser fix that is queued only reaches one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix reaches <SplitCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports.
<SplitCount ticket={4471} except="parser" /> reports stay broken after it ships: they are two
other bugs, and nobody is working on them.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

Every report is counted once: the ticket system exports in overlapping pages, so some reports
appear twice in the export. The table is built from the export when the page renders, so it
stays right as the desk merges more reports in. A problem's ticket says *not filed yet* until
it has an issue of its own.

## `parser` · the shared parser reads the month first

**What people see.** A booking lands on the wrong date, with day and month swapped: 7 April
goes out as 4 July. It happens on every channel: the app, email and the form.

**Why.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants in day-first locales
(Germany, the Netherlands, Poland, the UK and others) write the day first, so their dates
come out flipped.

**The rule.** The tenant's locale writes the day before the month, and the report did not
come in through a booking-v3 form. This is `causeOf` in `causes.ts`, unchanged.

<SplitTable ticket={4471} problem="parser" />

## `hint` · the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first

**What people see.** The same swap, day and month, but only in bookings made through the
booking form, and only for tenants on booking-v3.

**Why.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People do what the hint says
and type the month first. The form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The
parser is not involved: fix the parser and these bookings still come out wrong. The fix is
the hint (or the form reading what its hint asks for).

**The rule.** A day-first tenant on booking-v3, and the report came in through the form.
This is the `hint` branch of `causeOf`.

<SplitTable ticket={4471} problem="hint" />

## `one-day` · the date moves by one day, not a swap

**What people see.** The date is off by exactly one day: Tuesday shows as Monday. Day and
month are where they should be. Two people invited to the same meeting from different time
zones can get different days (`R-41344`: a client in Lisbon got Tuesday, one in Toronto got
Monday).

**Why.** This is a time zone or day boundary problem: a time is turned into a date in the
wrong zone and falls on the other side of midnight. It has nothing to do with the order of
day and month, so neither the parser fix nor the hint fix touches it.

**The rule.** Tenant and channel can't show this one. The desk finds it by reading what the
reporter wrote, and lists those reports in `desk-notes.json` (`oneDayMove`). A report on
that list is `one-day`, before any other rule. Today the #4471 page files these under
`parser` with a footnote; they belong apart.

<SplitTable ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

## Why one ticket hid three problems

The desk merges by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The
subjects here are things like "Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the invite" and "Month and day
swapped?", and each of those turns up under all three problems. A subject says how
the reporter felt, not what broke. Merging on it put all three problems in one place, and
the biggest one gave the ticket its name. [Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause)
proposes merging by cause instead; 0001 stays as it was written.

## What to do with #4471

- Keep #4471 for `parser`. The queued fix is right for it.
- Track `hint` and `one-day` each in their own issue (linked in the table above when filed),
  and stop merging new reports of either into #4471.
- When the parser fix ships, close only `parser`. Reports that keep coming in after that are
  most likely one of the other two.

